WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.774
Welcome to the Poly Firmware Academy. This first video decodes the two job postings for your team by following one real thing: what happens when our video bar is plugged into a laptop.

00:00:11.174 --> 00:00:17.850
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:24.485
Every new word gets a card on screen with a plain meaning and an everyday picture, so nothing is left undefined.

00:00:25.685 --> 00:00:33.847
Picture this. A sales team walks into a meeting room, plugs one USB C cable from the video bar into a laptop, and opens Zoom.

00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:36.477
The laptop has never seen this bar before.

00:00:36.727 --> 00:00:44.297
Yet within two seconds, Zoom lists a new camera, a new microphone and a new speaker, and the mute button on the bar works.

00:00:44.547 --> 00:00:53.057
Nobody installed a driver. In this video we open the box and follow that magic, piece by piece, because that path is exactly your job.

00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:57.928
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:00:58.978 --> 00:01:03.331
First, one picture to hang everything on. Think of the bar as a restaurant.

00:01:03.731 --> 00:01:08.201
The diners are the apps, like Teams or Zoom. They only ever see the dining room.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:13.617
The dining room is Android's system services, the layer the postings call the framework layer.

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:21.772
Framework layer: On Android, the Java system services apps talk to, like the camera and USB services. Like: The dining room of a restaurant: diners (apps) only ever see this room.

00:01:22.022 --> 00:01:28.814
The kitchen is where hardware gets turned into features: the hardware services and helper programs. That's your code.

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:33.359
The basement wiring is the Linux kernel and its drivers. Also yours.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:40.481
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:01:40.731 --> 00:01:44.945
The front door lock is the boot sequence and security checks. Also yours.

00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:53.623
And the delivery door is USB: how video, sound and button presses leave the building. The USB posting says this door is a must have.

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:59.978
Now the main walk. We'll follow the path from the laptop into the bar, one piece at a time.

00:02:00.378 --> 00:02:08.331
Piece one: the laptop. In USB the laptop is the host. It is always the one asking questions; the bar only ever answers.

00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:16.730
Piece two: the USB C cable. It carries power and data, and a couple of extra wires the two sides use to agree who is the host.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:33.733
Piece three: inside the bar's main chip sits a UDC, a USB device controller: the hardware that lets the bar answer the laptop. On our Qualcomm and NXP chips it is usually a DWC3, a ready-made controller design that Synopsys sells to chip makers.

00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:40.205
UDC: USB Device Controller: the chip block that lets a gadget answer a laptop over USB. Like: The front desk of a hotel: every guest request (the laptop) arrives there first.

00:02:40.505 --> 00:02:48.574
DWC3 (Synopsys IP): A ready-made USB controller design that Synopsys sells and many Qualcomm and NXP chips include. Like: A front desk bought from a furniture catalogue: many hotels have the same model, so one driver fits them all.

00:02:48.874 --> 00:03:02.980
Piece four: Linux's gadget framework, the device side of USB that lets the board pretend to be a webcam. We describe the costume with configfs: a special folder tree where making folders and writing small files builds the gadget.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:10.218
Gadget (Linux USB gadget): Linux's name for the device side of USB: the board pretends to be a webcam, mic or keyboard to a laptop. Like: An actor in costume: the same board can play "webcam" or "keyboard" depending on the costume it wears.

00:03:10.518 --> 00:03:18.424
configfs: A special folder tree where you build a USB gadget by creating folders and writing small files. Like: Building with LEGO by making folders: each folder is a brick (a function), and linking them snaps the toy together.

00:03:18.724 --> 00:03:39.750
Piece five: the functions. UVC, the USB video class, makes the bar a webcam. UAC2, the USB audio class, makes it a microphone and speaker. HID, the human interface class, carries the mute and call buttons. Every laptop already speaks these standard languages, so no driver install is ever needed.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:47.162
UVC: USB Video Class: the standard "I am a webcam" language every computer already understands. Like: A universal power plug for cameras: plug in anywhere and it just works, no driver install.

00:03:47.462 --> 00:03:52.977
UAC / UAC2: USB Audio Class (version 2 is the modern one): the standard "I am a mic or speaker" language. Like: The same universal plug idea as the webcam language, but for sound.

00:03:53.277 --> 00:03:59.581
HID: Human Interface Device: the standard language for buttons, keyboards and mice, also used for mute and call buttons. Like: The universal remote-control language: any computer knows what "button pressed" means.

00:03:59.881 --> 00:04:08.252
Piece six: a normal program on the bar, the U V C streaming app. When the laptop asks for video, this app answers with frames.

00:04:08.652 --> 00:04:19.252
Piece seven: where frames come from. The camera sensor and image processor deliver frames through V4L2, video for Linux, the standard Linux camera interface.

00:04:19.652 --> 00:04:24.412
V4L2: Video for Linux 2: the kernel interface every Linux camera and video device uses. Like: The standard loading dock every camera truck backs into.

00:04:24.712 --> 00:04:39.689
Piece eight: the Android USB HAL, a hardware abstraction layer: a fixed interface Android calls and we implement. It decides whether the port is in conferencing, file transfer or debug mode, and switches the gadget setup to match.

00:04:40.089 --> 00:04:47.090
HAL: Hardware Abstraction Layer: a fixed interface Android calls, which the device maker implements for its hardware. Like: A standard wall socket: Android plugs into the same socket, and each maker wires the house behind it.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.801
Let's watch one moment in detail: Zoom opening the camera.

00:04:52.201 --> 00:04:57.739
On plug in, the laptop runs enumeration: it asks the bar for its descriptors.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:05.383
Enumeration: The first conversation after plug-in where the laptop learns what the device is and gives it an address. Like: A new employee's first morning: show ID, get a badge number, hand in a resume, get assigned a desk.

00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:08.872
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:05:09.122 --> 00:05:14.567
The bar answers: I am a webcam, a microphone, a speaker, and some buttons.

00:05:14.967 --> 00:05:19.054
Zoom asks the webcam to start video at a certain size and format.

00:05:19.454 --> 00:05:23.564
The gadget framework passes that request up to the streaming app as an event.

00:05:23.964 --> 00:05:26.727
The app starts the camera at exactly that format.

00:05:27.127 --> 00:05:29.658
The camera hands back a filled frame buffer.

00:05:30.058 --> 00:05:33.448
The app queues the frame to the video endpoint on the controller.

00:05:33.848 --> 00:05:39.990
Endpoint: A numbered mailbox on a USB device; each data stream uses its own. Like: Numbered pigeonholes in an office: mail for video goes in one slot, audio in another.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:48.216
And the controller sends it as isochronous packets, a slice every hundred and twenty five microseconds. That's how a picture appears in Zoom.

00:05:48.616 --> 00:06:01.538
Isochronous transfer: A USB transfer type with guaranteed timing but no retries, used for live video and audio. Like: A live TV broadcast: always on time, and a dropped frame is simply skipped, never resent.

00:06:02.638 --> 00:06:07.468
The posting's phrase full USB software stack also includes three more rooms.

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:16.459
Host side: the bar also plugs in its own accessories, like extension microphones, so it runs an x H C I host controller too.

00:06:16.859 --> 00:06:30.338
xHCI: Extensible Host Controller Interface: the standard USB host controller in laptops and in our boards when they act as host. Like: The hotel manager who calls guests, as opposed to the front desk that answers them.

00:06:30.638 --> 00:06:38.835
Type C and power delivery: deciding who powers whom, which way the cable is flipped, and sending DisplayPort video over the same connector.

00:06:39.235 --> 00:06:50.880
USB Type-C: The reversible USB connector; it also negotiates power and roles through its extra pins. Like: A smart socket that works either way up and agrees on who powers whom before turning on.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:07:01.640
PD (Power Delivery): USB Power Delivery: the conversation over the Type-C cable that agrees on voltage, current and roles. Like: Two people agreeing how much to pay before handing over goods.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:08.117
Firmware updates over USB using D F U, including the small microcontrollers inside accessories.

00:07:08.517 --> 00:07:19.477
DFU: Device Firmware Upgrade: a standard way to send new firmware into a device over USB. Like: A car driving onto a service ramp so the mechanic can swap the engine software.

00:07:19.777 --> 00:07:23.666
MCU: Microcontroller: a small, low-power chip with its own memory, running firmware without a full operating system. Like: A pocket calculator compared with a laptop.

00:07:23.966 --> 00:07:31.071
And the debugging tools: usbmon, Wireshark and hardware analyzers. Lessons ten to fourteen cover each room.

00:07:31.471 --> 00:07:40.074
usbmon: A Linux feature that records every USB packet so you can read them in Wireshark. Like: A security camera pointed at the USB cable.

00:07:41.174 --> 00:07:43.647
Zooming back out, here's the whole stack.

00:07:44.047 --> 00:07:46.985
At the top, apps like Zoom. Not your layer.

00:07:47.385 --> 00:07:51.135
Android's framework services, which call down into your code.

00:07:51.535 --> 00:07:56.446
The hardware services and helper daemons, written in C plus plus. Yours.

00:07:56.846 --> 00:08:04.984
The kernel drivers: camera, audio, USB gadget, and I two C drivers for chips like the Lontium HDMI bridges. Yours.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:16.739
I2C: A simple two-wire bus that lets a processor talk to small chips like sensors and HDMI bridges. Like: A classroom where the teacher calls each student by number and they answer one at a time.

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:25.491
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:08:25.791 --> 00:08:28.287
The bootloader and secure boot. Yours.

00:08:28.687 --> 00:08:35.305
Bootloader: The small program that runs first at power-on, sets up memory and loads the kernel. Like: The stagehand who sets up the stage before the actors arrive.

00:08:35.555 --> 00:08:44.483
Secure boot / chain of trust: Each boot stage checks the digital signature of the next before running it. Like: Each guard checks the next guard's badge before handing over the keys.

00:08:44.783 --> 00:08:51.458
And the silicon from Qualcomm, NXP, Ambarella and Lontium, delivered with each vendor's BSP.

00:08:51.858 --> 00:09:04.885
BSP: Board Support Package: the chip vendor's bundle of bootloader, kernel, drivers and settings for one chip or board. Like: The starter kit that comes with a new appliance: parts, manual and tools for that exact model.

00:09:05.985 --> 00:09:09.189
Finally, the senior behaviors both postings repeat.

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:12.898
Lead secure, high performance C and C plus plus.

00:09:13.298 --> 00:09:16.201
Define architecture and standards with other teams.

00:09:16.601 --> 00:09:20.513
Troubleshoot the bugs that cross hardware, kernel and Android.

00:09:20.913 --> 00:09:24.268
Own planning, documentation and the test strategy.

00:09:24.668 --> 00:09:29.301
And mentor junior engineers. Lesson twenty turns these into weekly habits.

00:09:30.501 --> 00:09:38.721
Question for you: When the bar shows up in Zoom with no driver install, which two standard USB languages make the camera and the microphone work?

00:09:44.021 --> 00:09:53.657
Answer: The USB Video Class makes it a webcam, and the USB Audio Class makes it a microphone and speaker. Every laptop already speaks both.

00:09:54.757 --> 00:09:55.651
Quick recap.

00:09:56.051 --> 00:09:58.826
The laptop is the host and asks every question.

00:09:59.226 --> 00:10:12.554
Inside the bar, the path runs from the UDC, often a DWC3, through the gadget framework and configfs, to the U V C, U A C two and H I D functions, fed by the streaming app and the camera.

00:10:12.954 --> 00:10:16.042
Standard classes mean no driver install on any laptop.

00:10:16.442 --> 00:10:19.774
The Android USB HAL picks which mode the port is in.

00:10:20.174 --> 00:10:28.545
And senior means leading, setting standards and mentoring. Next up: the C and C plus plus habits firmware reviewers look for.
